{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.Deriv.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.Deriv.Pow","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.Deriv.Mul","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.Deriv.Add","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.Deriv.Inv","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.IteratedDeriv.Lemmas","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.ContDiff.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity"],"declarations":55,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_frames_agree_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_frames_agree_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":1469,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1469-L1472","doc":"**The two frames agree at $q = 1$.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_eq_grouped_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_eq_grouped_mul","kind":"theorem","line":1462,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1462-L1467","doc":"**The exact relation between the two frames**: canonical $\\cdot\\,q^6 =\n-P(q)\\cdot$ published. With `chi_block_21_P_at_one` this is the whole content of\n\"the orderings differ away from $q = 1$ and nowhere at it\". ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_canonical_has_q2_minus_1_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_canonical_has_q2_minus_1_factor","kind":"theorem","line":1453,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1453-L1460","doc":"**The $(q^2-1)$ factor survives**, so\n`prop:qbeta-delta-tau-canonical-conditional-q2-minus-1-factor` is unaffected in\nsubstance — only the cofactor `P21` changes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_canonical_vanishes_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_canonical_vanishes_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":1448,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1448-L1451","doc":"**Vanishing at the classical point** survives the ordering change, so\n`chi_blocks_vanish_at_one` holds in the canonical frame too. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_P_palindromic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_P_palindromic","kind":"theorem","line":1441,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1441-L1446","doc":"**$P$ is palindromic**, $q^{12}P(1/q) = P(q)$ — i.e. bar-involution\nsymmetric. This is the consistency check on the derivation that depends on no\nnumeric pin. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_P_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_P_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":1436,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1436-L1439","doc":"**$P(1) = -1$.** This single value is why the classical-point results are\nframe-independent: it makes the two forms agree exactly at $q = 1$. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_eq_canonical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_eq_canonical","kind":"theorem","line":1430,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1430-L1434","doc":"Since 2026-09-07 `chi_block_21` **is** the canonical form; this records the\nidentification so the name introduced by `qou-eddc` stays meaningful. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_canonical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_canonical","kind":"def","line":1423,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1423-L1428","doc":"Per-block character $\\chi_{(2,1)}(\\Delta_{T_\\beta})$ under the **canonical\n(borromean)** intra-nucleon ordering:\n\n$$\\chi_{(2,1)} = \\frac{(q^2-1)(q^4+1)\\,P(q)}{4q^9}.$$ ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_P","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_P","kind":"def","line":1419,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1419-L1421","doc":"The ordering-correction factor $P(q)$ relating the two frames. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.unique","kind":"theorem","line":1358,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1358-L1396","doc":"**Theorem (uniqueness of `ZRecursionCoeffs` instances).** Any two\ninstances of `ZRecursionCoeffs` have the same coefficient sequence.\n\nThis is a structural consequence of the recursion: the initial values\n`a_0`, `a_1` plus the linear recursion determine every `a_n` uniquely.\nProof: strong induction on `n`; base cases discharged by `initial_0`\nand `initial_1`; step uses the recursion at `n = k+1` to solve for\n`a (k+2)` in terms of `a (k+1)` and `a k`, then applies IH.\n\n**Status**: sorry-free. Strong induction on `n`; base cases discharged\nby `initial_0`/`initial_1`; step solves the recursion at `n = k+1` for\n`a (k+2)` using the IH on `a (k+1)` and `a k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.ZRecursionCoeffs.recursion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.ZRecursionCoeffs.recursion","kind":"theorem","line":1355,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1355-L1355","doc":"The 3-term recursion (proven from the q-difference equation\nfor `z`; sketched in the prearchimedean witness). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.ZRecursionCoeffs.initial_1","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.ZRecursionCoeffs.initial_1","kind":"theorem","line":1352,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1352-L1352","doc":"`a_1 = 0` (the first derivative of `z(q)` at `q = 1` vanishes\nby symmetry under `q ↔ 1/q`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.ZRecursionCoeffs.initial_0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.ZRecursionCoeffs.initial_0","kind":"theorem","line":1349,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1349-L1349","doc":"`a_0 = 1/2` (from `z(1) = √1 / (1+1) = 1/2`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.ZRecursionCoeffs.a","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.ZRecursionCoeffs.a","kind":"def","line":1347,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1347-L1347","doc":"The Taylor coefficient sequence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.ZRecursionCoeffs.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.ZRecursionCoeffs.mk","kind":"ctor","line":1345,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1345-L1345","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.ZRecursionCoeffs","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.ZRecursionCoeffs","kind":"class","line":1332,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1332-L1356","doc":"**Class (3-term recursion for z(q) Taylor coefficients around q = 1).**\n\nThe Taylor coefficients `a_n` of `z(q) = √q / (1 + q)` expanded around\n`q = 1` satisfy the 3-term recursion\n  `4(n+1)·a_{n+1} + 6n·a_n + (2n−1)·a_{n−1} = 0`,  n ≥ 1,\nwith `a_0 = 1/2` and `a_1 = 0`. Source: the q-difference equation for\n`z(q)` (linear ODE with rational coefficients in q).\n\nFollowing §3b-cond, the recursion is axiomatised as a class so that\nthe (proven) extended z^k family recursions can consume it.\n\nSource: `qbeta_prearchimedean_z_recursion.witness.json` and\n`qbeta_c3_multiblock_recursion_attack.py` header. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_canonical_vanish_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_canonical_vanish_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":1309,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1309-L1319","doc":"**Theorem (conditional on `CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3`).** The canonical\nΔτ_M vanishes at the classical point `q = 1`.\n\n**Proof**: each per-block `chi_block_λ` vanishes at `q = 1` (by\n`chi_blocks_vanish_at_one`), so the y_λ-weighted sum vanishes term-by-term\nregardless of the (finite) values of `y_λ(1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_canonical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_canonical","kind":"def","line":1303,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1303-L1307","doc":"The canonical Δτ_M sum, conditional on a `CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3`\ninstance. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3.sum_dim_normalised","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3.sum_dim_normalised","kind":"theorem","line":1301,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1301-L1301","doc":"Normalisation: weighted sum of dimensions equals 1.\n`dim V_(3) = 1, dim V_(2,1) = 2, dim V_(1,1,1) = 1` ⇒\n`y_(3) + 2·y_(2,1) + y_(1,1,1) = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3.y111","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3.y111","kind":"def","line":1297,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1297-L1297","doc":"Canonical weight on the sign irrep `V_(1,1,1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3.y21","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3.y21","kind":"def","line":1295,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1295-L1295","doc":"Canonical weight on the 2-dim irrep `V_(2,1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3.y3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3.y3","kind":"def","line":1293,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1293-L1293","doc":"Canonical weight on the trivial irrep `V_(3)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3.mk","kind":"ctor","line":1291,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1291-L1291","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3","kind":"class","line":1276,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1276-L1301","doc":"**Class (canonical Wenzl–Markov weights for H_3(q)).**\n\nThis class axiomatises the canonical Wenzl–Markov weights `y_λ(q)`\nfor the n=3 Hecke algebra. Each field encodes a property the canonical\nweights satisfy. Following AGENTS.md §3b-cond, this class is the\nsingle point of conjectural input that downstream conditional\ntheorems (e.g. `delta_tau_M_canonical_vanish_at_one`) consume as\nan `[Instance]` hypothesis.\n\n**Conjectural status**: the existence of an instance that simultaneously\nsatisfies all four axioms below for the closed-form Wenzl–Markov\nweights is the open math. The closed forms involve `√q` via the\nMarkov parameter `z = markov_parameter q`. A concrete instance would\nencode them as `noncomputable def`s and verify each axiom; doing so\nrequires the Wenzl recursion's `√q` handling beyond `ring`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.markov_parameter_symmetric_form","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.markov_parameter_symmetric_form","kind":"theorem","line":1256,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1256-L1274","doc":"**Theorem (symmetric form of the Markov parameter).** For `q > 0`,\n`z(q) = 1 / (√q + 1/√q)`.\n\nThis is the alternative defining form used in the canonical Wenzl\nconstruction. Proof outline: clear denominators on both sides,\nmultiply through by `(1 + q) · √q`, and use `Real.mul_self_sqrt :\n√q · √q = q` for `q ≥ 0` to identify the cross-terms.\n\n**Status**: sorry-free. Cross-multiply, use `Real.mul_self_sqrt :\n√q · √q = q` for `q ≥ 0`, finish with `field_simp; linarith`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.markov_parameter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.markov_parameter","kind":"def","line":1231,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1231-L1254","doc":"The Markov parameter `z(q) = √q / (1 + q)`. Pure archimedean\nconstruction — depends on `Real.sqrt`. Source: Wenzl.\n\n**CONVENTION C, deliberately, and this is the declaration that says so.**\n`√q / (1 + q)` is exactly `z_C`; canonical is convention D,\n`z_D = q³ / (q² + 1)` (owner ruling 2026-08-15). Two reasons this site is\nnot migrated:\n\n* Its only consumer, `QBetaCanonicalMarkovWeightsInstance`, exists to prove\n  `CanonicalMarkovWeightsH3` INHABITABLE — so that the §3b-cond conditional\n  theorems are non-vacuous — and says in its own header that it is \"NOT a\n  claim that this is THE canonical Wenzl–Markov instance\". Inhabitation does\n  not need the canonical `z`.\n* `markov_parameter_symmetric_form` below proves\n  `√q / (1 + q) = 1 / (√q + 1 / √q)`, which is a TRUE identity about `z_C`.\n  Substituting `z_D` would falsify a sorry-free theorem, not migrate it.\n\nRe-deriving the C-frame objects under D is a separate tranche (bean\n`qou-cds1`); per `QOU.QBeta.decayLadder_zC_threeRow_ne_zero` a plain\n`z_C → z_D` substitution is not a valid move there either. Until this\ndocstring existed the site was the ONE `actionable` (undeclared) Lean\nconvention-C definition in the corpus — see `scripts/lean-markov-convention-audit.py`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_taylor_coeff_7","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_taylor_coeff_7","kind":"theorem","line":1207,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1207-L1214","doc":"**Theorem (order-7 Taylor coefficient).** The 7-th Taylor coefficient at\n`q = 1` of `delta_tau_M_surrogate` is `c_7`, via the iterated-Leibniz recurrence\non `f·D = N`, in the canonical (borromean) frame. Matches the regenerated\n`qbeta-c3-multiblock-recursion-attack` witness. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_taylor_coeff_6","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_taylor_coeff_6","kind":"theorem","line":1199,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1199-L1206","doc":"**Theorem (order-6 Taylor coefficient).** The 6-th Taylor coefficient at\n`q = 1` of `delta_tau_M_surrogate` is `c_6`, via the iterated-Leibniz recurrence\non `f·D = N`, in the canonical (borromean) frame. Matches the regenerated\n`qbeta-c3-multiblock-recursion-attack` witness. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_taylor_coeff_5","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_taylor_coeff_5","kind":"theorem","line":1191,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1191-L1198","doc":"**Theorem (order-5 Taylor coefficient).** The 5-th Taylor coefficient at\n`q = 1` of `delta_tau_M_surrogate` is `c_5`, via the iterated-Leibniz recurrence\non `f·D = N`, in the canonical (borromean) frame. Matches the regenerated\n`qbeta-c3-multiblock-recursion-attack` witness. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_taylor_coeff_4","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_taylor_coeff_4","kind":"theorem","line":1183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L1183-L1190","doc":"**Theorem (order-4 Taylor coefficient).** The 4-th Taylor coefficient at\n`q = 1` of `delta_tau_M_surrogate` is `c_4`, via the iterated-Leibniz recurrence\non `f·D = N`, in the canonical (borromean) frame. Matches the regenerated\n`qbeta-c3-multiblock-recursion-attack` witness. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_taylor_coeff_3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_taylor_coeff_3","kind":"theorem","line":874,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L874-L888","doc":"**Theorem (order-3 Taylor coefficient = −23/9).** The third Taylor\ncoefficient at `q = 1` of `delta_tau_M_surrogate` is `−23/9`\n(`= g'''(1)/3!`) in the canonical (borromean) frame, matching the regenerated\n`qbeta-c3-multiblock-recursion-attack.witness.json`. Its `−41/9` is the\ngrouped-frame value, now kept there under `grouped_frame_superseded`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv2_hasDerivAt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv2_hasDerivAt_one","kind":"theorem","line":738,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L738-L872","doc":"**Theorem (order-3 / third derivative value).** The explicit\nsecond-derivative function has derivative `−46/3` at `q = 1`:\n\n  `g'''(1) = −46/3`.\n\nComputed via the quotient rule on `g''(q) = BIGNUM/BIGDEN` with all pieces\nevaluated at `q = 1` (so every closing goal is numeric). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv2_eventuallyEq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv2_eventuallyEq","kind":"theorem","line":725,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L725-L735","doc":"**Theorem (derivative-of-`deriv` equality near 1).** The derivative of\n`delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv` eventually equals `delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv2`\nat `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv_hasDerivAt2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv_hasDerivAt2","kind":"theorem","line":619,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L619-L723","doc":"**Theorem (surrogate Δτ_M second derivative).** For all `q > 0`, the\nderivative of `delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv` at `q` equals\n`delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv2 q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv2","kind":"def","line":596,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L596-L616","doc":"The explicit **second-derivative** function of `delta_tau_M_surrogate`\n(the derivative of `delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv`), via the quotient rule on\n`g'(q) = U(q)/V(q)` with `U = N'·D − N·D'`, `V = D·D`:\n\n  `g''(q) = (U'·V − U·V') / V²`,  `U' = N''·D − N·D''`,  `V' = 2·D·D'`,\n\nwhere the cross terms in `U' = (N'·D − N·D')'` cancel.  `N''` and `D''`\nare the second derivatives of the numerator / denominator of\n`delta_tau_M_surrogate`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_taylor_coeff_2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_taylor_coeff_2","kind":"theorem","line":584,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L584-L594","doc":"**Theorem (order-2 Taylor coefficient = 5/6 via second derivative).**\nThe second Taylor coefficient at q = 1 of `delta_tau_M_surrogate` is `5/6`.\n\nPer the script's order-2 print: `Order 2:  5/6`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv_hasDerivAt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv_hasDerivAt_one","kind":"theorem","line":484,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L484-L582","doc":"**Theorem (order-2 / second derivative).** The explicit first-derivative\nfunction has derivative `5/3` at `q = 1`:\n\n  `g''(1) = 5/3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv_eventuallyEq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv_eventuallyEq","kind":"theorem","line":472,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L472-L482","doc":"**Theorem (derivative equality in a neighborhood of 1).** The derivative of\n`delta_tau_M_surrogate` eventually equals `delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv` at `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_hasDerivAt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_hasDerivAt","kind":"theorem","line":416,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L416-L470","doc":"**Theorem (surrogate Δτ_M derivative).** For all `q > 0`, the derivative of\n`delta_tau_M_surrogate` at `q` is equal to `delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_deriv","kind":"def","line":402,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L402-L414","doc":"The first-derivative function of `delta_tau_M_surrogate`, written\nexplicitly via the quotient rule:\n\n  `g'(q) = (N'(q)·D(q) − N(q)·D'(q)) / D(q)²`,\n\nwith `N'(q) = 34q¹⁶-32q¹⁵-75q¹⁴+…-12q²+10q+2` and\n`D'(q) = 40q⁹+36q⁸+32q⁷` the numerator and denominator derivatives. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_hasDerivAt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_hasDerivAt_one","kind":"theorem","line":337,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L337-L400","doc":"**Theorem (order-1 Taylor coefficient = −5/6).** The first derivative\nof `delta_tau_M_surrogate` at `q = 1` equals `−5/6`.\n\nPer the script's order-1 print: `Order 1:  -5/6`.\n\n**Status**: sorry-free. Standard quotient-rule derivative via\n`hasDerivAt_pow`, `HasDerivAt.add`, `HasDerivAt.sub`, `HasDerivAt.mul`,\n`HasDerivAt.div`, then `convert ... ; ring` + `norm_num` for the\nfinal numerical identity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_vanish_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_vanish_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":328,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L328-L335","doc":"**Theorem (order-0 Taylor coefficient).** `delta_tau_M_surrogate 1 = 0`.\n\nThis is the classical-point vanishing — the y_λ-weighted sum\nof three per-block characters that each have a `(q^2-1)` factor\nmust vanish at `q = 1`. Per the script: `coeffs[0] = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_eq_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate_eq_sum","kind":"theorem","line":309,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L309-L324","doc":"**Theorem (surrogate Δτ_M closed form).** For all `q > 0`, the\ny_λ-surrogate-weighted sum of the per-block characters equals the\nrational closed form `delta_tau_M_surrogate q`. Pure ring identity\nafter clearing denominators. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.one_plus_q_plus_q_sq_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.one_plus_q_plus_q_sq_pos","kind":"theorem","line":304,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L304-L307","doc":"Helper: `1 + q + q² > 0` for any `q : ℝ` (sum of squares form). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.delta_tau_M_surrogate","kind":"def","line":284,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L284-L302","doc":"Multi-block Δτ_M closed form under the rational-y_λ surrogate.\n\nSource (SymPy): `qbeta_c3_multiblock_recursion_attack.py` Step 1.\n\nNumerator: `2q¹⁷ − 2q¹⁶ − 5q¹⁵ + 4q¹⁴ + 8q¹³ − 9q¹² − 8q¹¹ + 9q¹⁰ + 11q⁹ − 11q⁸\n − 9q⁷ + 8q⁶ + 9q⁵ − 8q⁴ − 4q³ + 5q² + 2q − 2`\nDenominator: `4 q⁸ (q² + q + 1)`\n\nRestated 2026-09-07 for the canonical **borromean** `chi_block_21` (bean\n`qou-eddc`); the grouped-frame form was `−q¹¹ + q¹⁰ − 2q⁹ + q⁸ + q⁶ − q⁵ − q³\n+ 2q² − q + 1` over `4q⁵(q²+q+1)`. The two agree at `q = 1` (both vanish) and\non the order-1 and order-2 Taylor coefficients; they part company at order 3.\n\nFor all `q > 0`, this equals `y_(3)·χ_(3) + y_(2,1)·χ_(2,1) + y_(1,1,1)·χ_(1,1,1)`\n(theorem `delta_tau_M_surrogate_eq_sum` below). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.y_lambda_surrogate_111","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.y_lambda_surrogate_111","kind":"def","line":280,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L280-L282","doc":"Per-block Wenzl–Markov weight surrogate `y_{(1,1,1)}(q) = 1 / ((1+q)(1+q+q²))`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.y_lambda_surrogate_21","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.y_lambda_surrogate_21","kind":"def","line":276,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L276-L278","doc":"Per-block Wenzl–Markov weight surrogate `y_{(2,1)}(q) = q / ((1+q)(1+q+q²))`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.y_lambda_surrogate_3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.y_lambda_surrogate_3","kind":"def","line":265,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L265-L274","doc":"Per-block Wenzl–Markov weight surrogate `y_{(3)}(q) = q² / ((1+q)(1+q+q²))`.\n\nNOT canonical — the canonical weight involves `√q` (via the Markov\nparameter `z = √q / (1+q)`). The surrogate is retained because it\npreserves the per-block dimension structure `{q², q, 1} / ((1+q)(1+q+q²))`\nand is sufficient to expose the NEAR-MATCH cross-anchor diagnostic.\nThe CANONICAL version is axiomatised in §Extension #4 below.\nSource: `qbeta_c3_multiblock_recursion_attack.py::y_lambda_h3_symbolic`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_blocks_have_q2_minus_1_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_blocks_have_q2_minus_1_factor","kind":"theorem","line":204,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L204-L241","doc":"**Theorem (q²−1 factor in all three blocks).** Every per-block\ncharacter $\\chi_\\lambda(\\Delta_{T_\\beta})$ factors as $(q^2 - 1)$\ntimes a rational function of $q$ on the physical regime $q > 0$\n(so the classical limit $q \\to 1^+$ is the structural source of\nthe vanishing — see `chi_blocks_vanish_at_one`).\n\nThe identity $q^2 - 1 = (q-1)(q+1)$ would algebraically extend the\nvanishing to $q = -1$ as well, but this theorem is stated under\n$q > 0$ to match the physical regime of $\\chi_\\lambda$ (where the\n$1/q^k$ denominators in the closed forms are well-defined and\npositive); the $q = -1$ root is outside scope.\n\nThis is the structural source of the order-$w$ leading term in the\nTaylor expansion around $q = 1$: $q^2 - 1 = 2w + w^2$ where\n$w = q - 1$. Each block contributes at first order in $w$, with\nthe per-block ε¹-order coefficients $\\{-2, -1, -2\\}$ from the\nwitness's `taylor_coefficients_per_block_ε_order` field. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_antipode_pairing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_antipode_pairing","kind":"theorem","line":185,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L185-L202","doc":"**Theorem (antipode pairing).** Under the substitution $q \\mapsto 1/q$\nthe trivial-irrep character $\\chi_{(3)}$ maps to the sign-irrep\ncharacter $\\chi_{(1,1,1)}$:\n\n  $\\chi_{(1,1,1)}(q) = q^{-4} \\cdot \\chi_{(3)}(q)$.\n\nThis reflects the standard Hecke-algebra identity\n$V_{(3)} \\otimes V_{\\text{sign}} = V_{(1,1,1)}$, with the $q^{-4}$\nprefactor coming from the framing weight (degree-$4$ shift between\nthe $q^{-1}$- and $q^{-5}$-denominators).\n\nProof: direct rational arithmetic; clear denominators and apply\n`ring`. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:qbeta-per-block-chi-closed-forms","label":"prop:qbeta-per-block-chi-closed-forms","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-endomorphism/qbeta-per-block-chi-closed-forms.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_blocks_vanish_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_blocks_vanish_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L173-L183","doc":"**Theorem (vanishing at $q = 1$).** Each per-block character\n$\\chi_\\lambda(\\Delta_{T_\\beta})$ vanishes at the classical point $q = 1$.\n\nThis confirms $\\Delta\\tau_M(1) = 0$ regardless of the y_λ weights —\nthe classical limit is trivial for every block. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_111","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_111","kind":"def","line":162,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L162-L171","doc":"Per-block character $\\chi_{(1,1,1)}(\\Delta_{T_\\beta})(q)$ on the\nsign irrep $V_{(1,1,1)}$.\n\nClosed form: $-(q^2 - 1)(q^2 + 1)^2 / (4q^5)$.\n\nAntipode pair with `chi_block_3`: $\\chi_{(1,1,1)}(q) = q^{-4} \\cdot \\chi_{(3)}(q)$,\nformalised below as `q⁻¹ ^ 4 * chi_block_3 q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_grouped","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21_grouped","kind":"def","line":155,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L155-L160","doc":"The **superseded grouped-frame** $\\chi_{(2,1)}$, retained so the two\norderings stay comparable inside Lean (see `chi_block_21_eq_grouped_mul` below)\nand so the historical form is not merely a comment. It is not used by anything\ndownstream. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_21","kind":"def","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L78-L153","doc":"Per-block character $\\chi_{(2,1)}(\\Delta_{T_\\beta})(q)$ on the\n2-dim irrep $V_{(2,1)}$.\n\nClosed form: $(q^2 - 1)(-q^4 - 1) / (4q^3)$.\n\n⚠ **ORDERING-DEPENDENT — this is the GROUPED-frame form** (bean `qou-eddc`).\nOf the three blocks here, this is the only one the 2026-09-05 intra-ordering\nruling (`qou-hu1f`) can reach: `chi_block_3` and `chi_block_111` are the\none-dimensional irreps, where the generators act by scalars and therefore\ncommute, so no reordering of the word changes them — both were verified\nsymbolically to be IDENTICAL under either ordering. $V_{(2,1)}$ is the only\n2-dimensional block of $H_3(q)$, and it is exactly where $\\sigma_0$ and\n$\\sigma_1$ fail to commute.\n\nUnder the canonical (borromean) word the character is\n\n$$\\chi_{(2,1)}(\\Delta_{T_\\beta}) = \\frac{(q^2-1)(q^4+1)\\,P(q)}{4q^9},\n  \\qquad P(q) = 2q^{12}-5q^{10}+5q^{8}-5q^{6}+5q^{4}-5q^{2}+2,$$\n\nrelated to the form below by the exact factor $-P(q)/q^6$, with $P(1) = -1$ and\n$P$ palindromic. Derivation, and a cross-check against a second implementation\nagreeing to ~1e-40:\n`computations/qbeta/qbeta_per_block_chi_borromean_closed_forms_probe.py`.\n\n**Nothing at the classical point changes.** $P(1) = -1$ makes the two forms\nagree exactly at $q = 1$; the $(q^2-1)$ factor, the limit\n$\\lim_{q\\to1}\\chi_{(2,1)}/(q^2-1) = -1/2$ and bar-oddness in $h = q - 1/q$ all\nsurvive (degree 3 → 9). `chi_block_vanishing_at_one`, the `(q^2-1) * P21`\nfactorisation and the order-1/order-2 Taylor coefficients (−5/6 and 5/6) are\nframe-independent; the first divergence is at order 3.\n\n**Migration attempted 2026-09-07 and REVERTED.** Swapping the definition\nrequires re-proving the whole derivative tower — `P21`,\n`delta_tau_M_surrogate`, both explicit derivative functions and three\n`HasDerivAt` chains whose numerator goes degree 11 → 17. That attempt left 16\ntactic errors in a file that compiles CLEANLY at 0 errors as written, so it was\nbacked out rather than landed broken. Tracked in `qou-eddc`; the mathematics is\nsettled and only the Lean plumbing remains.\n\n⚠ **ORDERING-DEPENDENT — this is the GROUPED-frame form** (bean `qou-eddc`).\nOf the three blocks in this file, this is the only one the 2026-09-05\nintra-ordering ruling (`qou-hu1f`) can reach. `chi_block_3` and\n`chi_block_111` are the one-dimensional irreps, where the generators act by\nscalars and therefore commute, so no reordering of the word changes them —\nboth are IDENTICAL under either ordering, verified symbolically. $V_{(2,1)}$ is\nthe only 2-dimensional block of $H_3(q)$, and it is exactly where $\\sigma_0$\nand $\\sigma_1$ fail to commute, which is the ruling's own justification.\n\nUnder the canonical (borromean) word the character is\n\n$$\\chi_{(2,1)}(\\Delta_{T_\\beta}) = \\frac{(q^2-1)(q^4+1)\\,P(q)}{4q^9},\n  \\qquad P(q) = 2q^{12}-5q^{10}+5q^{8}-5q^{6}+5q^{4}-5q^{2}+2,$$\n\nrelated to the form below by the exact factor $-P(q)/q^6$, with $P(1) = -1$\nand $P$ palindromic.\n\n**Nothing at the classical point changes.** Because $P(1) = -1$ the two forms\nagree exactly at $q = 1$; the $(q^2-1)$ factor, the limit\n$\\lim_{q\\to1}\\chi_{(2,1)}/(q^2-1) = -1/2$, and bar-oddness in $h = q - 1/q$\nall survive (degree 3 → 9). So `chi_block_vanishing_at_one`, the\n`(q^2-1) * P21` factorisation and the downstream Taylor data are unaffected in\nsubstance — only `P21` itself would change.\n\nThe definition is left in the grouped frame **deliberately**, until the\nmigration can be done as one piece: it carries proof obligations here and is\nreferenced by seven other files. That work is tracked in `qou-eddc`.\nDerivation and all eight checks:\n`computations/qbeta/qbeta_per_block_chi_borromean_closed_forms_probe.py`.\n\nStructural note: this collapses to LINEAR in $h = q - 1/q$ — the\n$b^2$ off-diagonal contributions from $\\sigma_1$ and\n$\\sigma_0\\sigma_1$ cancel (cf. `qbeta_per_block_symbolic.py`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chi_block_3","kind":"def","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/QBetaPerBlockChiClosedForms.lean#L69-L76","doc":"Per-block character $\\chi_{(3)}(\\Delta_{T_\\beta})(q)$ on the trivial\nirrep $V_{(3)}$.\n\nClosed form: $-(q^2 - 1)(q^2 + 1)^2 / (4q)$.\nSource: `qbeta-per-block-paper-closed-forms.witness.json`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}